.SH A) OVERVIEW
a script is a independently runable set of unit-test steps that have a common,
clear focus and a managable length.  scripts are the primary organizational
entity in unit testing.

.SH B) TECHNICAL
each script is ultimately translated as a single c-language function within
the program to test a specific unit/file.

.SH C) EXAMPLE
all unit testing scripts must begin with the SCRP verb, and are a single,
un-indented line containing five columns using ascii field-seperator (0x1c)
delimiters. these field delimiters will be shown here with the (§) character.

   verb--- § ---description----------- § dur § ---label--- § ---functions--- §
   SCRP    § (CFLOW) verify parsing    §  5s § parse       § parse_handler   §

.SH D) VERB
SCRP is the verb and must have zero indentation.  this field is a maximum of
twelve (12) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.

   verb--- § ---description----------- § dur § ---label--- § ---functions--- §
   SCRP    § ·                         § ·   § ·           § ·               §

.SH E) DESCRIPTION
it is very unhelpful to leave off a description as unit-testing is almost
invariably detailed, complicated, and extensive.  descriptions are a maximum
of sixty-five (65) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are letters, numbers, and most punctuation.

   verb--- § ---description----------- § dur § ---label--- § ---functions--- §
   SCRP    § (CFLOW) verify parsing    § ·   § ·           § ·               §

i usually prefix the description with a reference to the c source-file being
tested in all capitals, in this case "polymnia_cflow.c".  it just helps me
focus more quickly under stress ;)

.SH F) DURATION
many unit tests can load extensive data or run complicated algorithms,
so here i include a advisory duration in seconds (s), minutes (m), ....
many prepatory tests are likely 0s ;) durations are a maximum of three (3)
characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.  legal characters
are [0-9] followed by my standard time-units [smhdwoyc].

   verb--- § ---description----------- § dur § ---label--- § ---functions--- §
   SCRP    § ·                         §  5s § ·           § ·               §

.SH G) LABEL
my vim-based ide (vide) picks up these labels to provide an index on the
left-side of my editing window for rapid access.  labels are a maximum
of fourteen (14) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are [a-zA-Z0-9_].  again, helps with my focus and speed.

   verb--- § ---description----------- § dur § ---label--- § ---functions--- §
   SCRP    § ·                         § ·   § parse       § ·               §

.SH H) FOCUS
this is a comma separated list of the primary functions under test for
strictly advisory and reporting purposes.  the function list is a maximum of
seventy-five (75) characters, overflow and trailing spaces will be truncated.
legal characters are [a-zA-Z0-9_, ].

   verb--- § ---description----------- § dur § ---label--- § ---functions--- §
   SCRP    § ·                         § ·   § ·           § parse_handler   §

.SH I) SEQUENCING
koios and ouroboros can group unit-test scripts into a natural sequence where
all dependencies are used to create a clean test order.  the seqencing is 
appended to the SCRP verb and has there parts...
   a) major = stage       [0-9]    highest ordering withint program
   b) minor = wave        [a-z]    sub-order within stage
   c) plus  = criticality [A-Za-z] pre-defined, specific options

the final is six (6) characters, ex. "[1a/b]" surrounded by square brackets.

   verb--------- § ---description----------- § ´´´
   SCRP   [1a/b] § ·                         § ´´´

this field is critical to maintaining massive code bases.

.SH J) OUTPUT
when the unit-test is run, two (2) specific outputs are generated for each
script -- a header before its condent and a footer after.

when a script is reached, the output will indicate a script start,
that it is the 4th in the current unit-test, and that the source data is on
line 315 of the unit test script.  the line mumber is very, very useful.
it also shows the expected duration, label, and focus.

   ===[[ NEW SCRIPT ]]======================================================
   SCRP [04] (CFLOW) verify parsing ==========================[1a/b]=[00315]
     5s  : parse          : parse_handler

when all steps in a script are complete, a footer appears with script specific
statistics.  PRCS is SCRP backward ;) the footer says their where 47 tests and
45 passed with zero failures.  this line will also be "green" if all passed or
"red" with any failed for fast visual scanning.

   ···PRCS  ------- cond=10  test=47  [ pass=45  fail=0   badd=0   void=2  ]


